# TahoePilot Project Conventions

## Documentation

- All docs are written in **English**, including the roadmap (`docs/ROADMAP.md`).
- Do not add other parallel locale files beyond this roadmap pair.
- Layout: `README.md` (overview, quick start, doc index), `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` (system map, core concepts, design principles), and one `README.md` per subsystem next to its code (`agent/`, `agent/specialists/databot/`, `tools/`, `config/`, `memory/`, `rag/`, `evals/`, `frontend/`). A subsystem README covers concepts, design, how it works, usage, extension and pitfalls; link to other subsystem docs instead of repeating them.
- When code changes behavior, paths, commands or config keys, update the matching subsystem README in the same change.

## Code

- Python at a strong professional bar: clear typing, single-purpose modules, reuse existing abstractions; avoid over-engineering.
- Comments, docstrings, CLI output, and error messages describe **behavior and usage only** — not iteration labels, roadmap items, optimization narratives, or AI conversation context.
- Keep changes scoped to the task; no drive-by refactors.

## Optimization log (`docs/CHAT_OPTIMIZATION.md`)

After a meaningful optimization, append a section **above** `<!-- Append the next optimization section above this line -->`. Do not create separate long-form write-ups.

Section shape:

1. Heading: `## YYYY-MM-DD — short title`
2. One-line **Goal**
3. Table of **important optimization points** (not files changed or code details)

| Optimization | Before | After |
|--------------|--------|-------|

Writing rules:

- Stay concise; the file grows over many rounds — keep each section short.
- Before / After: about one line each — enough to convey problem and outcome.
- Product/architecture level only — no file lists, phases, or diffs.
- No AI meta-talk or project-management labels; describe what changed and why in plain terms.

## Roadmap

Planning and milestone tracking live in `docs/ROADMAP.md`; optimization detail lives in `CHAT_OPTIMIZATION.md`. Neither belongs in code.

## New features

When the user asks to add a new feature, ask whether to create a GitHub issue before creating one. Create the issue only after they say yes.
